# HTTP API layer

- `routes.go` is the only place routes are declared: method, path under `/api/v1`, access level (`AccessPublic`, `AccessDeveloper`, `AccessSession`, `AccessAdmin`), handler and optional `maxBody`. `register` panics on a missing level and puts the route's level in the request context (`routeAccessFrom`); `routes_test.go` pins every route's level (`expectedRoutes`) and runs the identity-by-route matrix. Handlers never check roles; the only per-resource rule is `authorizeJob` (restricted jobs, cancel by creator).
- Handlers have the signature `func(w, r) error` and return `*apiError` values (`respond.go`) or plain errors, which become 500 `internal_error` with the cause logged under the request id and never sent. An error that is only the request's own context being cancelled (`context.Canceled` in the chain and `r.Context()` done; `canceledByCaller`) is not a failure: nothing is written and the access log records it once at debug level with status 499 (`StatusClientClosedRequest`; also for handlers that return without writing after the client left). A dependency's own timeout (`context.DeadlineExceeded`) stays an error; a cancellation during daemon shutdown is logged as a warning and answered 503. Decode bodies with `decodeJSON` (strict, media type checked, body limit gives 413).
- `dispatch` sends `/api/...` to the mux and renders its 404 and 405 as JSON; everything else goes to the SPA handler.
- `outer` middleware: request id (`X-Request-Id`), client address (`resolveClientIP` in `netpolicy.go`: TCP peer, or the right-most non-trusted `X-Forwarded-For` entry when the peer is in `Config.TrustedProxies`; read it with `clientIP(r)`), access log (no query strings, bodies or credentials), panic recovery, security headers (strict CSP for the SPA, `default-src 'none'` and `no-store` for the API, HSTS only with TLS), and the Host allowlist (IP literals, `localhost`, `Config.PublicHosts`; otherwise 421 `bad_host`, logged at most once a minute). Tests that build requests with `httptest.NewRequest` rely on the harness allowing `example.com`.
- `Server.Serve` uses explicit timeouts; streaming handlers extend their write deadline per chunk with `http.ResponseController` and stop on client disconnect or `s.shutdown`. Upload handlers must extend the read deadline the same way.
- `Deps.Servers` and `Deps.Static` back the server/image/volume and static routes (see the servers rule); when nil those routes answer 503. `Deps.Builds` (`*build.Service`) backs starting and deleting builds (see the builds rule); listing builds needs only the store.
- Start jobs from handlers with `s.startJob` (busy subject gives 409 `job_in_progress`, shutdown gives 503) and respond 202 with the job (encrypted columns use `s.secrets` with `secrets.ProjectAuthPurpose(id)` or `secrets.ServerEnvPurpose(id)`); jobs started from admin routes (or outside the route table) are `restricted`. Following log streams take `s.acquireLogStream(r)` (4 per account, 429). Encrypted columns use `s.secrets` with `secrets.ProjectAuthPurpose(id)` or `secrets.ServerEnvPurpose(id)`.
- Password hashing and checks only through `passwords.go` (see the auth rule). `CheckListen` resolves `localhost` and requires every address to be loopback.
- Verify with `go test ./internal/httpapi/...`.
